# 34. Better Long-Term Approach: Database Role

For a company application, instead of individually granting permissions to every stored procedure, you can create a database role.

For example:

```sql
USE Dapper;
GO

CREATE ROLE DapperApiRole;
GO

GRANT EXECUTE TO DapperApiRole;
GO

ALTER ROLE DapperApiRole
ADD MEMBER [IIS APPPOOL\Dapper_CRUD];
GO
```

However, whether `GRANT EXECUTE` on the whole database is appropriate depends on your company's security policy.

### More restrictive approach

Grant only the procedures required by the application:

```sql
GRANT EXECUTE ON dbo.sp_GetEmployee TO DapperApiRole;
GRANT EXECUTE ON dbo.sp_GetEmployeeById TO DapperApiRole;
GRANT EXECUTE ON dbo.sp_AddEmp TO DapperApiRole;
GRANT EXECUTE ON dbo.sp_UpdateEmployee TO DapperApiRole;
GRANT EXECUTE ON dbo.sp_DeleteEmployee TO DapperApiRole;
```

For production systems, **least privilege is preferable**.

