## The key lesson from our deployment issue

The most important part of the troubleshooting we did is this:

```text
HTTP 500
   ↓
Check IIS/Application logs
   ↓
SQL Exception 229
   ↓
EXECUTE permission denied
   ↓
Check IIS identity
   ↓
IIS APPPOOL\Dapper_CRUD
   ↓
Create SQL Login
   ↓
Create/verify Dapper database user
   ↓
Grant EXECUTE permission
   ↓
Recycle Application Pool
   ↓
Test API
```

So **IIS itself was not fundamentally broken** in your case. The application was successfully starting and reaching SQL Server. The failure was that the IIS Application Pool identity did not have permission to execute the stored procedures.

For a company SOP, I would keep this document as the **standard deployment procedure**, and add a separate **"IIS Deployment Troubleshooting Guide"** containing the common `500`, `403`, `404`, `405`, connection-string, runtime, permissions, and SQL authentication errors.
